\begin_layout Section
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consumer theory begins with feasible choice and observable demand.
 This chapter introduces the commodity space,
 budget sets,
 demand,
 Walras' law,
 homogeneity of degree zero,
 the weak axiom of revealed preference,
 and the Slutsky matrix.
 The next chapter develops the abstract connection between choice and preference;
 the utility-maximization chapter then joins these two perspectives.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Environment:
 Commodities and Budget Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An economic commodity is a good or service whose quantity matters to the consumer.
 There are 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq1$
\end_inset

 commodities.
 A consumption bundle is a column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 denotes the quantity of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset
.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 records the bundles that are feasible for the consumer.
 Unless a section states otherwise, we take 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, so negative consumption is ruled out.
 Other applications may impose upper bounds or interpret coordinates as net trades.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The consumer cannot generally choose every bundle in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 In the benchmark model, prices and wealth are the only constraints.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 denote wealth and let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 denote the price vector.
 The budget set is 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

, a subset of the consumption set.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Demand Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After specifying the consumer's environment, we take demand as the observable primitive.
 A demand function is a map 
\begin_inset Formula $x:\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}\to X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for every price--wealth pair; equivalently, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)\leq w$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\ell}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 denotes the quantity of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

 consumed at prices and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand describes behavior; it does not yet explain that behavior through preferences or optimization.
 Treating the entire function as observed is an idealization, because an empirical dataset contains only finitely many price--wealth observations.
 We therefore distinguish restrictions on a full demand function from tests that can be implemented on finite data.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Cobb-Douglas Demand Function.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)=(\frac{\alpha w}{p_{1}},\frac{(1-\alpha)w}{p_{2}})^{\top}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Consistency in Consumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the environment and demand primitive in place,
 we turn to consistency in consumption choices.
 This approach restricts observable behavior before imposing a specific preference representation.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will impose a set of restrictions or axioms over the Marshallian demand function that we expect to hold in all situations irrespective of the details.
 Here we understand modeling consumption as restricting the set of allowable behaviors.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These axioms are minimal theoretical requirements,
 not empirical laws that every dataset must satisfy.
 Their value is that they make the set of admissible behavior explicit and provide a baseline against which richer models can be compared.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Walras' law) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to satisfy Walras' law if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

 for all price-wealth pairs.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Walras' law imposes budget exhaustion;
 it rules out choices that leave unspent wealth when a locally nonsatiated consumer could purchase more.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Homogeneity of degree zero, HD0.) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero if, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
HD0 says that multiplying all prices and wealth by the same positive scalar does not change demand.
 It is a normalization of units,
 not a behavioral claim about intelligence.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Weak axiom of revealed preference, WARP.) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if, for any two price--wealth pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

, whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{*},w^{*})\leq w$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{*}$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WARP excludes a two-observation reversal: if the bundle chosen at the second budget was affordable at the first, then the distinct bundle chosen at the first must be unaffordable at the second.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an observed choice 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{t},x^{t})$
\end_inset

, define direct revealed preference by 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}R^{D}y$
\end_inset

 means that the observed bundle cost at least as much as 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 at the prices under which 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}$
\end_inset

 was chosen:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
x^{t}R^{D}y\iff p^{t}\cdot x^{t}\geq p^{t}\cdot y.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For distinct chosen bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, WARP is equivalently 
\begin_inset Formula $zR^{D}y\Longrightarrow\neg(yR^{D}z)$
\end_inset
.
 This is a restriction on observed choices; it need not be interpreted as a primitive psychological preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Comparative Statics and Slutsky Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After establishing the axioms of behavior,
 the next step is to study their implications for the parameters of the model.
 The parameters of the model in our study of consumer behavior are prices and wealth pairs.
 Assume throughout this section that the demand function is continuously differentiable on an open domain of strictly positive prices and wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The price Jacobian is 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}$
\end_inset

;
 its 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ell,k)$
\end_inset

 entry is 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{p_{k}}x_{\ell}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}x(p,w)=(\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w))_{\ell=1}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be the vector of wealth effects.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Slutsky matrix is 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

.
 It measures the derivative of demand under a price change that adjusts wealth just enough to keep the previously chosen bundle affordable.
 Its 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ell,k)$
\end_inset

 entry is 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\ell k}=\partial_{p_{k}}x_{\ell}(p,w)+\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)x_{k}$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix a reference bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and consider the compensated demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,p\cdot q)$
\end_inset

.
 The chain rule gives 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}[x(p,p\cdot q)]=D_{p}x(p,p\cdot q)+D_{w}x(p,p\cdot q)q^{\top}$
\end_inset

.
 At 
\begin_inset Formula $q=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, Walras' law gives 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot q=w$
\end_inset

, so this derivative is exactly the Slutsky matrix:
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of Walras' Law for the Slutsky Matrix

\end_layout

\begin_layout Fact
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then
 (i) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}D_{w}x(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

,
 (ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}D_{p}x(p,w)=-x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

,
 (iii) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}S(p,w)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part (i) implies that at least one good has a positive wealth effect.
 Because prices are strictly positive and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\ell}p_{\ell}\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

, there must be some 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)>0$
\end_inset
.
 In general,
 Walras' law restricts the wealth effects,
 while part (iii) restricts consumption changes under Slutsky-compensated price changes.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of Homogeneity of Degree Zero.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and homogeneity of degree zero, then 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

, homogeneity gives 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\lambda p,\lambda w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0$
\end_inset

.
 Differentiating with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=1$
\end_inset

 yields

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
D_{p}x(p,w)p+D_{w}x(p,w)w=0.
\]

\end_inset

 Walras' law gives 
\begin_inset Formula $w=p\cdot x(p,w)=x(p,w)^{\top}p$
\end_inset

; therefore the left-hand side is 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p$
\end_inset
, as required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of WARP.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main implication of the WARP while assuming Walras' law is the Compensated Law of Demand (CLD).

\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
(CLD) We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Compensated Law of Demand if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*}-p)\cdot(x(p^{*},w^{*})-x(p,w))<0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{*},w^{*})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{*}=p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We next establish that WARP implies the compensated law of demand.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfy Walras' law and WARP. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the CLD.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix two price--wealth pairs satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{*}=p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{*},w^{*})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset
.
 Set 
\begin_inset Formula $z=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y=x(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Walras' law and the compensation condition give 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot z=w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot z=p^{*}\cdot y=w^{*}$
\end_inset
.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot z\leq w^{*}$
\end_inset

, WARP and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\neq z$
\end_inset

 imply 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y>w$
\end_inset
, hence 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot(y-z)>0$
\end_inset
.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Combining this strict inequality with 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot(y-z)=0$
\end_inset

 gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
(p^{*}-p)\cdot(y-z)<0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The differential version yields the following corollary.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable and satisfies Walras' law and WARP.
 Then, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and every price-wealth pair, 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\top}S(p,w)a\leq0$
\end_inset
.
 Equivalently, the symmetric part 
\begin_inset Formula $[S(p,w)+S(p,w)^{\top}]/2$
\end_inset

 is negative semidefinite.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Fix a price-wealth pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and a direction 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset
.
 For sufficiently small 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, define 
\begin_inset Formula $p(t)=p+ta$
\end_inset
 and compensate wealth by 
\begin_inset Formula $w(t)=p(t)\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset
.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
t a\cdot[x(p(t),w(t))-x(p,w)]\leq0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\frac{a\cdot[x(p(t),w(t))-x(p,w)]}{t}\leq0
\]

\end_inset

 for every nonzero sufficiently small 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 after dividing the revealed-preference inequality by 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{2}>0$
\end_inset
.
 Continuous differentiability and the chain rule imply
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\lim_{t\to0}\frac{x(p(t),w(t))-x(p,w)}{t}
=D_{p}x(p,w)a+D_{w}x(p,w)\,x(p,w)^{\top}a
=S(p,w)a.
\]
\end_inset
 Taking limits gives 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\top}S(p,w)a\leq0$
\end_inset
.


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout









\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Marshallian and compensated price effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Slutsky decomposition separates the total response of demand to a price change from the response at fixed purchasing power.  For a differentiable demand function,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\partial_{p_k}x_\ell(p,w)=S_{\ell k}(p,w)-x_k(p,w)\,\partial_w x_\ell(p,w).
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under WARP and Walras' law the compensated matrix has a negative-semidefinite symmetric part.  Thus a positive Marshallian own-price derivative \begin_inset Formula $\partial_{p_\ell}x_\ell>0$\end_inset
 is possible only when the income effect is sufficiently adverse: the good is inferior (\begin_inset Formula $\partial_w x_\ell<0$\end_inset
), and the income effect dominates the nonpositive compensated effect.  This is the Giffen mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The possibility of a Giffen response does not by itself establish a utility representation.  Two distinct rationalizability routes are developed later.  For a differentiable demand system, local integrability uses Walras' law, homogeneity, Slutsky symmetry and negative semidefiniteness together with explicit domain and regularity hypotheses.  For a finite budget dataset, observed expenditure exhaustion and GARP provide the Afriat certificate.  The compensated response, rather than the Marshallian derivative alone, is the object restricted by revealed preference.
\end_layout


