\begin_layout Section
Measurement Error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A similar experiment of budget allocation but using 3 goods instead of 2,
 is in Ahn,
 D.,
 Choi,
 S.,
 Gale,
 D.,
 & Kariv,
 S.
 (2014) (Estimating ambiguity aversion in a portfolio choice experiment.
 Quantitative Economics,
 5(2),
 195–223).
 This experiment has 
\begin_inset Formula $n=154$
\end_inset

 individuals,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T=50$
\end_inset

 trials.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The GARP test is successful 
\begin_inset Formula $12.99\%$
\end_inset

 of cases (individuals).

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Aguiar and Kashaev (2020,
 Restud) extend GARP to allow for measurement error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Measurement error can be either present in consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=x_{t}^{*}+w_{t}^{c}$
\end_inset

 or in prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}=p_{t}^{*}+w_{t}^{p}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}^{*},p_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 being the correctly measured prices and consumption.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GARP should hold for the (ideal),
 well-measured dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{t}^{*},x_{t}^{*})_{t=1}^{T}$
\end_inset

 but not necessarily for the mismeasured dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{t},x_{t})_{t=1}^{T}$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Without any knowledge about measurement error then rationality becomes untestable.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We assume that measurement error is random across individuals and that the expectation is equal to zero:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{c}]=0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{p}]=0$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
These assumptions are backed up by validation experimental data that shows that consumers may incur in trembling hand errors,
 or perception errors that are not systematic (see our paper).

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The key is that we do not know the distribution of measurement error,
 we only know it's average behavior.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Aguiar and Kashaev (2020) provide a methodology to test GARP in the ideal (correctly measured data) with knowledge about average behavior of measurement error.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
They conclude that the null hypothesis that every individual in this experiment is consistent with GARP cannot be rejected at the 95 confidence level.
 They also find that misperception of prices seems to be the culprit of spurious violations of rationality.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Does this confirm rationality?
 NO!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A theory cannot be confirmed by this exercise alone,
 but current evidence is insufficient to reject rationality (at least in the context of this simple experiment).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Welfare Evaluation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will compare the wealth needed to achieve a certain level of utility at a given price-wealth pair.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
u^{1}=v(p^{1},w)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
u^{0}=v(p^{0},w).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are interested in comparing the expenditure needed to achieve 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{0}$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a fixed reference price and a given utility level 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

,
 the needed expenditure to reach it is given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
e(p^{ref},u).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus the comparison in terms of expenditure is given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
e(p^{ref},u^{1})-e(p^{ref},u^{0})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
e(p^{ref},v(p^{1},w))-e(p^{ref},v(p^{0},w)).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This expression will be positive when it takes more wealth to achieve 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{1}$
\end_inset

 at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{ref}$
\end_inset

 than to achieve 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{0}$
\end_inset

.
 This difference is measured in dollar terms.
 That is why 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{ref},v(p,w))$
\end_inset

 is often called money metric indirect utility function.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can construct many money metric indirect utility functions with different reference price.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{ref}=p^{0}$
\end_inset

 is the original price situation, the expenditure change equals the wealth change that makes the consumer indifferent between the new price with the old wealth and the old price with the new wealth.
 This asks what wealth change is equivalent to the price change.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalent variation is
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\operatorname{EV}(p^{0},p^{1},w)=e(p^{0},v(p^{1},w))-e(p^{0},v(p^{0},w))=e(p^{0},v(p^{1},w))-w,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{0},v(p^{0},w))=w$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this sign convention, equivalent variation is the money change at the reference price that is equivalent to the price change: it is willingness to pay for an improvement, and its negative is willingness to pay to avoid a worsening.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other case considered is the one where the new price is taken as the reference price,
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{ref}=p^{1}$
\end_inset

,
 the expenditure change equals the wealth change that makes the consumer indifferent between the original situation 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{0},w)$
\end_inset

 and the new situation 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{1},w+\Delta w)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Compensating variation is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\operatorname{CV}(p^{0},p^{1},w)=e(p^{1},v(p^{1},w))-e(p^{1},v(p^{0},w))=w-e(p^{1},v(p^{0},w)).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sign convention for compensating variation is analogous: a negative value for a worsening is the compensation required to restore the initial utility.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Homothetic and Quasilinear Utility Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Homothetic preferences.
 A preference relation is homothetic if \begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$\end_inset
 implies \begin_inset Formula $\alpha x\succeq\alpha y$\end_inset
 for every \begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$\end_inset
 whenever the scaled bundles are feasible.
 Under the usual continuity and monotonicity assumptions, it admits a positively homogeneous utility representation; an arbitrary representation need not itself be homogeneous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Cobb--Douglas representation \begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2})=x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}$\end_inset
 is a standard example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Quasilinear preferences.
 A numeraire good \begin_inset Formula $L$\end_inset
 is quasilinear when preferences admit a representation
\begin_inset Formula
\[
u(x)=g(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L-1})+x_{L}.
\]
\end_inset
 Equivalently, translating both bundles by the same amount of the numeraire does not change their ranking whenever both translated bundles remain feasible.
\end_layout


