\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\max_{0\leq\alpha\leq w}\int u(w+\alpha(z-1))dF(z).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first-order conditions are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\phi(\alpha^{*})=\int u'(w+\alpha^{*}[z-1])[z-1]dF(z)\begin{cases}
\leq0 & if\quad\alpha^{*}<w\\
\geq0 & if\quad\alpha^{*}>0.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\int zdF(z)>1$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(0)>0$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $u'(w)>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(0)=\int u'(w)[z-1]dF(z)$
\end_inset

.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 cannot satisfy the first order conditions.

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a risk is actuarially favorable, then a risk-averse expected-utility maximizer will accept at least a sufficiently small amount under the stated differentiability and interior-wealth assumptions.

