\begin_layout Section
From accounts to counterfactuals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Computable general equilibrium (CGE) analysis uses a calibrated microeconomic model and a social accounting matrix to evaluate policy counterfactuals.
 Calibration chooses parameters so that the model reproduces a benchmark allocation and the associated accounting identities before the policy is changed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A two-sector small open economy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The benchmark economy has two goods,
 aggregate labor \begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset
,
 and sector-specific capital \begin_inset Formula $K_j$
\end_inset
.
 A small open economy takes world prices as given.
 For sector \begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
,
 a constant-elasticity-of-substitution production function is
\begin_inset Formula
\[
Q_j=A_j\left[\alpha_jK_j^{-\rho_j}+(1-\alpha_j)N_j^{-\rho_j}\right]^{-1/\rho_j},
\]

\end_inset
 with \begin_inset Formula $A_j>0$
\end_inset
,
 \begin_inset Formula $0<\alpha_j<1$
\end_inset
,
 and \begin_inset Formula $\rho_j>-1,\ \rho_j\ne0$
\end_inset
.
 The associated elasticity parameter is \begin_inset Formula $\sigma_j=1/(1+\rho_j)>0$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
(Cost and conditional factor demand.)
 Given output \begin_inset Formula $\bar Q_j$
\end_inset
 and factor prices \begin_inset Formula $(r_j,W)$
\end_inset
,
 the unit-consistent cost function is
\begin_inset Formula
\[
c_j(r_j,W,\bar Q_j)=\min_{K_j,N_j\geq0}\{r_jK_j+WN_j:Q_j(K_j,N_j)\geq\bar Q_j\}.
\]

\end_inset
 When differentiable,
 conditional factor demands satisfy
\begin_inset Formula
\[
K_j^D=\frac{\partial c_j}{\partial r_j},\qquad N_j^D=\frac{\partial c_j}{\partial W}.
\]

\end_inset
 This derivative formulation avoids confusing the price \begin_inset Formula $P_j$
\end_inset
 with the CES parameter \begin_inset Formula $\rho_j$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Social accounting and calibration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The social accounting matrix records production,
 factor payments,
 household income,
 and expenditure flows that must balance at the benchmark.
 For a Cobb--Douglas expenditure equation \begin_inset Formula $P_jQ_{h,j}^D=\beta_{h,j}Y_h$
\end_inset
,
 the observed benchmark share identifies \begin_inset Formula $\beta_{h,j}=P_jQ_{h,j}^D/Y_h$
\end_inset
.
 The calibrated model is checked by substituting the parameter values back into every accounting identity and market-clearing condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Equilibrium closure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A computable model is not defined by technology and accounting identities alone.
 Let households be indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
 and sectors by 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
.
 Household income must specify factor ownership, taxes, transfers, and the disposition of profits and tariff revenue; a representative identity is
\begin_inset Formula
\[
Y_h=W\bar N_h+\sum_j r_j\bar K_{hj}+T_h+\Pi_h-\operatorname{Tax}_h.
\]
\end_inset
 Demand equations 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{hj}=x_{hj}(P,Y_h)$
\end_inset
 then close the household side.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each good, the resource balance distinguishes domestic output, imports, exports, intermediate use, household demand, investment, and government demand:
\begin_inset Formula
\[
Q_j+M_j=\sum_hx_{hj}+G_j+I_j+X_j+\sum_k a_{jk}Q_k.
\]
\end_inset
 Factor markets satisfy
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\sum_jN_j=\sum_h\bar N_h,
\qquad
K_j=\sum_h\bar K_{hj}
\quad\text{for sector-specific capital}.
\]
\end_inset
 Constant returns and competitive pricing impose zero profit, so the domestic producer price equals unit cost whenever the sector operates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government budget identifies the use of tax and tariff receipts, and the external account identifies the financing of net imports.
 A balanced-trade closure, for example, imposes
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\sum_j P_j^{W}M_j=\sum_j P_j^{W}X_j,
\]
\end_inset
 whereas a fixed foreign transfer replaces the right-hand side by exports plus that transfer.
 The model must choose one nominal normalization, such as 
\begin_inset Formula $W=1$
\end_inset
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{ER}=1$
\end_inset
, and must not impose both as independent equilibrium equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A benchmark replication check solves this entire system at the calibrated parameters and reports the maximum absolute residual in every household, firm, market, government, and external-account equation.
 Counterfactual welfare must also be declared, for example equivalent variation relative to the benchmark utility and price system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Counterfactual policies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an imported good \begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset
,
 let \begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{ER}$
\end_inset
 denote the exchange rate and \begin_inset Formula $P_i^{W}$
\end_inset
 the world price.
 A tariff rate \begin_inset Formula $\tau_i\geq0$
\end_inset
 changes the domestic price according to
\begin_inset Formula
\[
P_i=\mathrm{ER}\,P_i^{W}(1+\tau_i).
\]

\end_inset
 Free trade is the baseline \begin_inset Formula $\tau_i=0$
\end_inset
;
 a tariff counterfactual changes \begin_inset Formula $\tau_i$
\end_inset
 and resolves the full equilibrium system.
 Other counterfactuals change tax,
 subsidy,
 transfer,
 or technology equations while leaving the accounting identities and calibrated preference and technology parameters in place.
\end_layout


